# TalkTally — Security & Access Document

## Overview
TalkTally prioritizes user privacy and data security while maintaining simplicity for caregivers. This document explains authentication, authorization, data protection, permissions, and incident response for TalkTally.

## Authentication & Authorization
### Device-Based Authentication (MVP)
- On first launch the app generates a UUID v4 and stores it locally (AsyncStorage or SecureStore).
- This deviceId is sent with API requests to associate sessions to a device.
- No username/password in MVP — anonymous tracking only.

### Future: Account & Family Sharing
- Phase 2 will provide optional account creation (email + password) and family sharing with proper authentication (bcrypt hashed passwords, JWT or session tokens).

## Data Classification & Handling
### Data Collected
- Device ID (UUID) — Stored locally and on server for mapping sessions
- Session metadata — accuracy, total_steps, session_number, completed_at
- App version & OS type for analytics

### Data Not Collected (MVP)
- No personally-identifiable information (PII): names, emails, phone numbers
- Audio recordings are never uploaded to the server (device-only)

### Storage & Retention
- Server: session metadata retained 24 months by default; policy may be configurable
- Device: recordings and profile data stored on-device; user controls retention

## Permissions & Runtime Handling
### Microphone Permission
- iOS: NSMicrophoneUsageDescription in Info.plist with clear rationale
- Android: RECORD_AUDIO in AndroidManifest
- Request flow: requestRecordingPermissionsAsync() is called at calibration time; if denied, present a rationale and link to settings

### Storage Permission (if needed)
- If the app writes to shared storage or external directories on Android, request WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE as required, with clear rationale

## Audio Handling & Security
- Audio recordings remain on-device and are saved in the app's private data directory
- Audio is not uploaded to any server in MVP; any future cloud backup is opt-in and encrypted in transit (TLS) and at rest

## Network & Data Transmission
- All backend communication occurs over HTTPS (TLS 1.2+)
- Backend hosted on Railway provides managed SSL certificates
- API endpoints: GET /health, POST /sessions, GET /sessions?deviceId=...

## Backend Security
- Use parameterized queries with pg to prevent SQL injection
- Limit returned data to the deviceId requested
- Enforce validation of deviceId format (UUID) on server-side
- Consider rate limiting on per-device basis (Phase 2)

## Database Security & Schema
- users table stores device_id (unique)
- sessions table stores per-session metadata and references users(device_id)
- Index sessions on device_id for efficient lookup

## Access Control
- No user accounts in MVP; deviceId acts as the access key for session retrieval
- For future authenticated accounts, implement RBAC and session ownership checks

## Logging & Monitoring
- Avoid logging sensitive data (deviceId considered non-PII but treated carefully)
- Log errors and server events; redact any potential PII
- Future: integrate with Sentry (mobile & backend) for crash reporting

## Incident Response
1. Contain: Isolate affected services
2. Assess: Determine scope and user impact
3. Notify: Follow regulatory timelines (GDPR 72-hour rule if applicable)
4. Remediate: Apply fixes, rotate secrets if needed
5. Post-Mortem: Publish findings and action items

## Compliance
- GDPR: minimal personal data collection, provide privacy policy and deletion options in future
- CCPA: allow users to request data deletion/exports (Phase 2)
- COPPA: app targets caregivers; do not collect child PII or upload audio. If future features collect child PII, add parental consent flows.

## Secure Development Practices
- Do not store secrets in repository; use environment variables in Railway
- Run dependency audits (npm audit) and keep dependencies up to date
- Use CI checks for linting and TypeScript type safety

## Future Security Enhancements (Roadmap)
- Use expo-secure-store or Keychain for deviceId storage
- Certificate pinning and request signing
- Rate limiting and IP-based anomaly detection
- Encryption of audio at rest (optional, user-configurable)
- Penetration testing and third-party security audit

